== Usage ==
{{Main|Leaves#Usage}}
Cherry leaves that decay have the same drop chances as when broken by players, yielding [[cherry sapling]]s 5% ({{frac|1|20}}) of the time, [[stick]]s 2% of the time, and otherwise nothing. Leaves that are burned do not drop saplings.

Unlike most other leaves, cherry leaves are not [[biome]] tinted and always stay pink. They also do not turn white during [[snowfall]].

=== Smelting ingredient ===
{{Smelting
|showname=1
|Cherry Leaves
|Leaf Litter
|0.1}}

=== Redstone component ===
{{Exclusive|java|section=1}}
The state of a leaves block—including a player-placed block—changes after 1 game tick (half a redstone tick) when the distance to the nearest [[log]] or [[wood]] block changes, up to 6 blocks of leaves away. [[Observer]]s facing away from the leaves detect this change and transmit a redstone signal in the same game tick, making leaves useful for redstone signal transmission.

=== Piston interactivity ===
Leaves are destroyed when pushed by [[piston]]s. They do not stick to [[sticky piston]]s, [[slime block]]s, or [[honey block]]s.

=== Composting ===
Cherry leaves have a 30% chance of increasing the compost level in a [[composter]] by 1. A stack of leaves yields an average of 3.05 [[bone meal]].

=== Bees ===
Cherry leaves function like any other [[flower]] in their interaction and uses with [[bee]]s.

=== Particles ===
[[File:Cherry leaves particle example JE.png|Particles falling from cherry leaves|thumb]]
Cherry leaves produce falling leaves [[particles]] underneath the leaf block, where they slowly fall to the ground. They are always pink like the leaves block. Cherry leaves particles are differently textured compared to other falling leaves, and appear more often.

